x_comment(x_FATFilesystem,"The collection of all FAT. A type of #$Filesystem. File Allocation Table (FAT) is the main file system developed for MS-DOS and Windows. The FAT file system is considered relatively uncomplicated, and because of that it is popular format for floppy disks. It is also often used to share data between several operating systems booting on the same computer. FAT is a relatively early file system design, and because of that it suffered from several problems. First, its simple file layout allows for fragmentation, which lead to significant slow-ups during the file system operation. Secondly, FAT was not designed for redundancy in the case of system failure. Thirdly, the first versions of FAT allowed file name sizes of only up to 11 characters. With #$MSWindowsNT operating systems, FAT began to be superceded by NTFS (#$NewTechnologyFilesystem). #$FATFilesystem is a #$FileSystemTypeByImplementation.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ec793).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ec793,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
