x_comment('x_containsInformationAbout-Focally',"This predicate relates sources of information to their focal topics.  <code>(#$containsInformationAbout-Focally IS THING)</code> means that <code>IS</code> is generally \"about\" <code>THING</code> -- which may be any instance of #$Thing at all. This predicate represents a very general notion of aboutness, which does not require that <code>THING</code> be explicitly named in <code>IS</code>, but that it be one of the main topics of <code>IS</code>. \n<p>\nNote that <code>IS</code> can have more than one focal topic -- e.g., a textbook can have both logic and set theory as main topics. Use the more specific predicate  #$containsInformationAbout-UniqueMainTopic to associate <code>IS</code> with its unique main topic. Use the more general predicate #$containsInformationAbout to specify that <code>IS</code> contains some information about <code>THING</code>, but that <code>THING</code> is not a main topic of <code>IS</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eced4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eced4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eced4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eced4,5721).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505eced4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eced4,[]).
x_comment('x_containsInformationAbout-UniqueMainTopic',"This predicate relates sources of information to their unique main topics. <code>(#$containsInformationAbout-UniqueMainTopic IS THING)</code> means that <code>IS</code> is specifically, focally, and mainly \"about\" <code>THING</code> -- which may be any instance of #$Thing at all.\n<p>\nNote that <code>IS</code> can have only exactly one unique main topic.  For example, a restaurant review that is about a single restaurant will have that restaurant as its unique main topic.  Often an <code>IS</code> will have several focal topics instead of a unique main topic.  In such cases, use the more general predicate #$containsInformationAbout-Focally.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eced5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eced5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eced5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eced5,5724).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505eced5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eced5,[]).
x_comment(x_nameString,"(#$nameString TERM STRING) means that the #$ProperNameString STRING names the thing denoted by TERM. Normally, STRING corresponds to a proper name. For a term that has more than one name associated with it, use #$preferredNameString for the name that is to be used in natural language paraphrase. Note that two name strings for a given thing need not have the same sense or intension; thus, for instance, (#$nameString #$MarkTwain \"Samuel Clemens\") holds.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eced6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eced6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eced6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
