xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed69c,[]).
x_comment(x_broaderTerm,"<code>(#$broaderTerm TERM1 TERM2)</code> means that <code>TERM2</code> is <i>broader</i> than <code>TERM1</code>, in an extremely general sense of \"broader\".  For example,  <code>TERM2</code> is, in the relevant sense, broader than <code>TERM1</code> if any of the following hold:<br><br>\n\n <code>TERM2</code> is a collection that subsumes <code>TERM1</code>; <br>\n <code>TERM2</code> is a collection that has <code>TERM1</code> as an instance; <br>\n <code>TERM2</code> is a collection, instances of which have instances of the collection <code>TERM1</code> as parts; <br>\n <code>TERM2</code> has <code>TERM1</code> as a part; <br><br>\n\nThis list of reasons for why <code>(#$broaderTerm TERM1 TERM2)</code> could hold is not exhaustive, but it should give a good indication of how general the #$broaderTerm relation actually is.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed69d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed69d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed69d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed69d,7750).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed69d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed69d,[]).
x_comment(x_SystemRolePredicate,"A specialization of #$Role.  Each instance of #$SystemRolePredicate is a binary predicate that relates an agent (an instance of #$Agent-PartiallyTangible) to a functional system (an instance of #$FunctionalSystem), specifying a functional role that the agent plays in the system.  Unless there are contextual or system-specific constraints to the contrary (cf. #$FixedFunctionalSystem), an agent may play multiple roles within the same system, and may indeed play different roles at different times in the history of the system.  Each instance of #$SystemRolePredicate is associated with a certain set of expectations (see #$expected-ToBe and #$amenable-ToBe) about how an agent who has a role will behave while playing the role, and also with a set of expected capabilities that the agent playing the role must have or maintain during the time it plays that role.  As a rule, these expectations may be satisfied by an agents's taking any number of actions. ") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed69e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed69e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed69e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed69e,7758).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed69e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed69e,[]).
x_comment(x_skillLevel,"This predicate relates performers to types of actions.  (#$skillLevel OBJ ACT-TYPE ROLE PERF-ATT LEVEL) means that the #$SomethingExisting OBJ has the ability to play the role ROLE in instances of the type of #$Event ACT-TYPE, with LEVEL degree of PERF-ATT.  For example, (#$skillLevel Joe #$DiggingAHole #$performedBy #$Agility #$highAmountOf) means that, in general, Joe can dig holes with great agility.  To talk about actual performance in a particular action, use #$performanceLevel.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed69f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed69f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed69f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed69f,7759).
