x_comment('x_terminalOfTrans-Generic',"(#$terminalOfTrans-Generic TRANS THING) means that THING marks one of the             terminal points in the instance TRANS of #$GeneralizedTransfer, either its             beginning or its end.  For example, consider a trip TRANS from Austin to             Omaha, NE.  In this case, it would correct to assert both             (#$terminalOfTrans-Generic TRANS #$CityOfAustinTX) and              (#$terminalOfTrans-Generic TRANS #$CityOfOmahaNE).  Compare this predicate to             #$to-Generic and #$from-Generic: #$terminalOfTrans-Generic is             a generalization of both of these predicates.  Note also that like #$to-Generic and #$from-Generic this predicate is very general.  Here are two more examples to illustrate its generality: (i) someone sends an e-mail in an instance EMailSending03 of #$EMailSending from the instance EMailAddress03 of #$EMailAddress to EMailAddress04.  In this case it would be correct to assert both (#$terminalOfTrans-Generic EMailSending03 EMailAddress03) and (#$terminalOfTrans-Generic EMailSending03 EMailAddress04).  (Note that in this case it would be better to use the more specific predicate #$informationTerminal.)  (ii) in the instance GAIN001 of #$GainingUserRights the instance AGT001 of #$Agent-PartiallyTangible receives something from AGT002.  In this case both (#$terminalOfTrans-Generic GAIN001 AGT001) and (#$terminalOfTrans-Generic GAIN001 AGT002) are correct.  You may also want to look at #$transferRouter-Generic and #$transferRelay-Generic.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed6cd).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed6cd,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed6cd,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
