x_comment(x_commonNickname,"A predicate which relates human names (considered as instances of #$LexicalWord) to character strings which represent conventional variants of those names. (#$commonNickname NAME NICKNAME-STRING) means that the #$HumanGivenNameString NICKNAME-STRING is a common nickname for the #$Name NAME. This predicate is to be distinguished from #$nicknames, which (somewhat indirectly) relates a nickname to the thing which has the nickname.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed722).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed722,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed722,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed722,8047).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed722,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed722,[]).
x_comment(x_FurniturePiece,"A specialization of #$DurableGoods, #$HumanOccupationConstructObject, \nand #$SolidTangibleArtifact.  Each instance of #$FurniturePiece is a \ndurable artifact of a type that is typically used in human dwellings \n(see #$HumanShelterConstruction) to make them more comfortable.  Many\nuseful specializations of this collection can be classified according \nto the main function their instances serve.  Such functions include: \n(i) providing support for people's bodies while they are seated or \nreclining (e.g. #$Bed-PieceOfFurniture and #$FootStool), (ii) providing \nwork or activity surfaces (e.g. #$Desk-PieceOfFurniture and \n#$DiningRoomTable), (iii) providing organized or protective storage \nfor one's belongings (e.g. #$Dresser-PieceOfFurniture and \n#$Bookcase), and (iv) providing illumination (e.g. \n#$ElectricLamp).  Most pieces of furniture are #$NonPoweredDevices \n(q.v.) in that they don't need any energy input in order to serve their \nfunction; but of course there are plenty of exceptions to this, such \nas electric lamps, console TVs, and hospital beds.  Note that \n#$FurniturePiece only includes _whole_ pieces of furniture; e.g. a \nsofa is an instance, but an arm of a sofa is not.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed723).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed723,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed723,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed723,8048).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed723,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed723,[]).
x_comment(x_ObjectControlFrame,"This frame is used for words that subcategorize for (i) an NP and (ii) an infinitive phrase which is controlled by the object of the matrix clause, i.e. the semantic subject of the infinitive clause is identical to the syntactic object of the matrix clause. Example: 'Mary convinced John to go to the party'. Note that this could be paraphrased as 'Mary convinced John that *John* (not Mary) go to the party', thus that the *object* (not subject) of the matrix clause is the one going to the party. Contrast this with #$TransitiveSubjectControlFrame.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed724).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed724,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed724,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed724,8066).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed724,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed724,[]).
x_comment(x_ViolentAction,"A specialization of #$HostileSocialAction.  Each instance of #$ViolentAction is a purposeful action of a type commonly considered violent -- generally because it is an action which intentionally attacks, damages, or destroys another agent, or attacks, damages, or destroys something close to some (target) agent. (This closeness may be physical or may consist in the fact that the agent owns or feels strong positive regard for the thing attacked.) Notable specializations of this collection include #$War, #$Torture and #$Bombing.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed725).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed725,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed725,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
