x_comment(x_importAmountDuring,"(#$importAmountDuring REGION PRODUCT-TYPE THING AMOUNT) means that the #$ScalarInterval AMOUNT is a measure of the amount of instances of the #$ProductType PRODUCT-TYPE imported by the #$GeographicalRegion REGION during the temporal extent of the #$TemporalThing THING.  THING will often be a calendar time period; but it may also be, for instance, a particular #$Event.  In the case of automobile imports, for example, AMOUNT might be an #$Integer indicating the number of cars imported.  In the case of oil imports, AMOUNT might be a #$Volume (a number of barrels) indicating the total quantity of oil imported, or it might be an instance of #$MonetaryValue indicating the monetary value of all the oil imported.  Alternatively, AMOUNT might be a #$Rate, characterizing the average rate of import of instances of PRODUCT-TYPE by REGION during the temporal extent of THING.  Oil imports, for example, might be expressed in terms of so many million barrels per day or in terms of so many dollars per day (again, indicating the monetary value of the oil).  The total value of a region's imports of all goods and services (over a given time period) can be expressed with this predicate using the constant #$Product as the second argument.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed739).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed739,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed739,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed739,8091).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed739,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed739,[]).
x_comment(x_DosageOfByAnimalTypeFn,"(#$DosageOfByAnimalTypeFn ?STUFF-TYPE ?ANIMAL) returns the collection of food ?STUFF-TYPE that is ingested by animals ?ANIMAL.  ?STUFF-TYPE is usually something edible.  Useful for talking about #$RelativeGenericValueFn statements e.g. `a lot of chocolate taken orally for a dog', `a small amount of meat taken orally for a person'.  This NAT was motivated by the need to say `if you are a person and drink a_lot_of_coffee_for_a_person, you'll be poisoned.' and `if you are a dog and eat a_lot_of_chocolate_for_a_dog, you'll be poisoned.' #$RelativeGenericValueFn requires these `a lot' collections to be reified and we decided a NAT was the best way to do this.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed73a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed73a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed73a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
