x_comment(x_InstrumentPlayingFn,"(#$InstrumentPlayingFn INSTRUMENT) denotes the collection of all events in which a single instrument of type INSTRUMENT is playing.  Thus, in a brass quintet playing there are five instances of (#$InstrumentPlayingFn #$BrassInstrument) occurring.  There may also be an overall event of the ensemble making music, but #$InstrumentPlayingFn cannot be used for the composite event, because that would violate the single-instrument constraint.  #$InstrumentPlayingFn is useful for telling Cyc that whenever a musical group, such as a marching band, is playing, there are (probably multiple) sub-events in which individual instruments are playing.  We further specify that each instance of each result of applying\n\n#$InstrumentPlayingFn be a #$temporallyContinuousDuring instance of\n\n#$MakingMusicalSound.  Thus the discontinuous event that was the\n\nmusical piece played by a particular trumpet in a performance of\n\nBeethoven's Ninth Symphony would consist of several continuous\n\ninstances of (#$InstrumentPlayingFn #$Trumpet) figuring as\n\n#$subEvents.  However, there is a granularity to what 'continuous'\n\nmeans in this context: specifically we are not including the miniscule\n\npauses that may obtain between the playing of one note and the next on\n\nthe instrument in question: thus, instances of the results of applying\n\n#$InstrumentPlayingFn may consist of musical playings of more than one\n\nnote.  Finally, collections reified by means of this function include only 'live' performances, not digital or analog recordings of performances.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed780).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed780,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed780,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
