x_comment(x_MerchandiseRelatedToFn,"#$MerchandiseRelatedToFn takes as argument any instance of #$Thing and returns the collection of products related in some relatively direct way to that thing.  Relevant relations include being designed, made, marketed or sold by that thing, and being about or inspired by that thing.  For example, (#$MerchandiseRelatedToFn #$NewEnglandPatriots) returns the collection of all products by, about, or in some way supporting the Patriots football team, and (#$MerchandiseRelatedToFn #$RepublicanPartyIdeology) returns the collection of all products about, inspired by, or in some way supporting Republican beliefs.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed82b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed82b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed82b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed82b,8504).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed82b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed82b,[]).
x_comment(x_WorldModelingProgramObject,"A specialization of #$ProgramObject.\nEach instance of #$WorldModelingProgramObject is the abstract structure of a representation\nof an instance of #$Thing.\nComputer programs are useful to humans because they are efficient processors and manipulators\nof information about things outside of the program that are interesting to humans . \nWhen this information, about some #$Thing, happens\nto be represented in a single instance of #$ComputerDatatypeType, then that instance is almost \nalways also a specialization of #$WorldModelingProgramObject. \nComputers were initially designed to handle representations of numbers only. \nBut with the advent\nof modern programming languages, complex user-defined datastructures allow the representation\nof almost arbitrary things, including instances of #$Set-Mathematical, #$Relation, \nor even #$Person. The collection #$WorldModelingProgramObject is useful in distinguishing those\ndata a program's user will care about from those that are merely implementation details.\nSee also the unary denotational function #$ProgramObjectValueFn and the binary predicate\n#$programObjectRepresentsValue.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed82c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed82c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed82c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed82c,8505).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed82c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed82c,[]).
x_comment(x_RegisteringAComplaint,"An instance of #$TemporalObjectType and a specialization of #$Complaining.  Each instance of #$RegisteringAComplaint is an event in which some agent <i>A</i> presents a complaint to some other agent <i>B</i>, where the following conditions are satisfied:\n<p>\n(1) <i>A</i> believes that some agent (perhaps, but not necessarily, <i>B</i>) is responsible for the state of affairs being complained about;\n<p>\n(2) <i>A</i> believes that <i>B</i> is in a position to remediate the state of affairs being complained about.\n<p>\nSome positive examples: complaining to a hotel front desk that the towels in one's room are dirty; complaining to one's supervisor at work that one hasn't been provided with sufficient resources to complete an assigned task; complaining to the #$BetterBusinessBureau that one has been swindled in a business scam.  Negative examples: complaining to one's doctor about one's constant back pain (condition 1 is not satisfied); complaining to one's friend that one is being treated poorly at work (condition 2 is not satisfied).") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed82d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed82d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed82d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed82d,8521).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed82d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed82d,[]).
x_comment(x_MinisterOfEnergy,"A collection of #$PublicOfficials that are head of the department(s) responsible for the energy production/distribution in a country. By (#$positionOfPersonInOrganization PERSON (#$GovernmentFn COUNTRY) #$MinisterOfEnergy) we say that PERSON has this position in COUNTRY. There may be more than one #$MinisterOfEnergy for a country at any one time, if the government structure is divides the responsibility among several officials, e.g. the minister of oil, the minister of nuclear energy, etc.. Also, a minister may have more responsibilities including 'energy', e.g. 'minister of infrastructure'. \n\nFinally, note that the title for the official in this capacity need not be 'minister', e.g. secretary, chancelor, etc.. ") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed82e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed82e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed82e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed82e,8528).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed82e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed82e,[]).
x_comment(x_PrivilegeEscalationScriptFn,"(#$PrivilegeEscalationScriptFn ACCOUINT ACCOUNT_TYPE) denotes a computer script which, when executed at a sufficient level of privilege, confers the privileges associated with ACCOUNT_TYPE onto ACCOUNT.  Thus, (#$PrivilegeEscalationScriptFn (#$UserAccountFn \"blake\" #$Pyrex-Cyc-Com) #$AdministratorAccount) denotes a (let's say) batch file which, when executed, confers all the privileges of an NT administrator account to someone logged in as #$Blake on #$Pyrex-Cyc-Com.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed82f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed82f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed82f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed82f,8531).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed82f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed82f,[]).
x_comment(x_toState,"This predicate is used in connection with #$IntrinsicStateChangeEvent, to identify the state (#$ObjectTypeByIntrinsicState) a thing has immediately after the change.  (#$toState EVENT STATE-COLL) means that following the intrinsic-state-changing action, EVENT, the object undergoing the change is an instance of STATE-COLL (which is an instance of #$ObjectTypeByIntrinsicState) and was previously an instance of some other instance of #$ObjectTypeByIntrinsicState.  For example, after any X which is a #$TurningOnAnElectricalSwitch, the switch involved has (#$toState X #$Device-On).") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed830).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed830,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed830,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed830,8532).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed830,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed830,[]).
x_comment(x_BillionDollarsPerYear,"A function which returns an element of #$MonetaryRate.  (#$BillionDollarsPerYear 1) equals (#$DollarsPerYear 1000000000).  Note, the dollars here are United States dollars. (#$Dollar-UnitedStates).") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed831).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed831,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed831,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
