x_comment(x_signifiesTo,"This is the most basic general signification or representation predicate.  (#$signifiesTo THING1 THING2 AGENT) means that, to AGENT, THING1 signifies THING2.  To AGENT's mind (or other system of semeiosis), the 'sign' THING1 represents, refers to, denotes, describes or depicts its 'object' THING2.  THING2 can be anything, and THING1 can be almost anything, although it is most often a word, phrase, description, string, image or symbol.  Very abstractly speaking,  AGENT must be capable of representation, or semeiosis, whereby AGENT's relationship to the the 'sign' THING1 causes AGENT to come into a related relationship with the 'object' THING2.  Note that this predicate does not state the mechanism by means of which THING1 represents THING2; that can be asserted with more specific predicates.  In order to omit the  explicit mention of the 'interpretant' or AGENT to whom THING1 means THING2, use the short-form #$BinaryPredicate #$signifies.  Often there is a convention (intersubjective understanding and agreement) by which one thing represents another, such as the word 'Dog' referring to dogs in English, or the abstract tetrahedron referring to the element Fire in #$Alchemy.  A pile of three rocks on a path can signify 'danger ahead' and the number thirteen can signify bad luck.  But there need be no specific convention; Deer tracks in the sand can signify the deer to the tracker, and a picture or photograph may signify what ever it is a picture of to anyone who beholds it, in both cases without any prior convention or agreement.  Thus this predicate has more specialized predicates #$signifiesTo-Icon (which represents by means of resemblance, like a picture or a stalactite), #$signifiesTo-Index (which represents by causation, as a deer track represents a deer or a weather vane represents the North Wind), and #$signifiesTo-Symbol (which represents by some convention, as 'dog' represents dogs by the conventions of English).  These are three main kinds of sign in the semiotic theory of #$CharlesPeirce.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed856).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed856,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed856,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed856,8596).
