x_comment('x_LinearObject-NonPlanar',"A specialization of #$LinearObject (q.v.). #$LinearObject-NonPlanar is the collection of all linearly-shaped objects, whether tangible or intangible, that are <i>not</i> embeddable (even roughly) in a #$Plane, and thus are only embeddable in a three-dimensional space region.  Since a #$LinearObject can itself be three-dimensional, we stipulate that non-planar 3-D a linear-object is not even embeddable in a #$FlatSurfaceRegion (q.v.).  \n<p>\nNote that the criterion for being \"non-planar\" in the present sense varies from context to context.  For example, the standard of non-planarity for the manufacture of wrought-iron railing is less precise than that for pure geometry.  \n<p>\nSpecializations of #$LinearObject-NonPlanar include #$Spiral.  And cf. #$LinearObject-Planar.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed86f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed86f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed86f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed86f,8641).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed86f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed86f,[]).
x_comment('x_collectionSalientAttribute-VirtuallyRepresented',"A #$CollectionSalientAttributePredicate and specialization of #$collectionSalientAttribute-Represented.  <code>(#$collectionSalientAttribute-VirtuallyRepresented COL ATTR)</code> means that <code>ATTR</code> is something (often a predicate, but possibly a function or collection-type) that is or corresponds to a salient attribute of <code>COL</code> that is at present <i>not</i> explicitly represented in the CycL language by a reified term, but that is readily representable by some reifiable CycL term constructable in the current CycL vocabulary.  Thus, anything in the extent of this predicate represents a straightforward opportunity to expand the expressivity of the CycL language in a useful way.\n<p>\nFor more details, see the specializations of this predicate, viz. #$collectionSalientDisjointWith, #$collectionSalientPartitions, #$collectionSalientSharedFeatureAssertions, #$collectionSalientFacetingBinPredVersion, and #$collectionSalientFacetingBinPredInverseVersion.\n<p>\nCf. #$collectionSalientAttribute-ActuallyRepresented and #$collectionSalientAttribute-Unrepresented.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed870).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed870,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed870,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed870,8646).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed870,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed870,[]).
x_comment(x_UniqueIDType,"Each element of #$UniqueIDType is a collection of ID objects (numbers, strings, etc.) which uniquely identify some class of things.  Some example instances of #$UniqueIDType include:  #$SocialSecurityNumber, whose elements each identify some #$Person; and 'GM vehicle ID number', whose elements would each identify some instance of #$GMAutomobile.  Note that #$SerialNumber-String is not an instance of this class, since it is possible for two manufacturers to use the same number or string to identify different individuals, but specializations of #$SerialNumber-String, such as 'AcmeSerialNumber', might be valid instances.  See also #$uniqueIDForType, which lets you state the class of objects which are uniquely identified by an ID type.  A good rule of thumb is that if you can't come up with a collection of objects that an ID type uniquely discriminates among, then it probably should not be an instance of #$UniqueIDType.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed871).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed871,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed871,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed871,8651).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed871,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed871,[]).
x_comment(x_LinearlyOrderedByStarts,"This is the collection of instances of #$ScriptedEventTypes, INS, that have the following feature: for every pair of distinct scenes SCENE1 and SCENE2 that are parts of INS, exactly one of (#$startsAfterStartOfInSituationType SCENE1 SCENE2) or (#$startsAfterStartOfInSituationType SCENE2 SCENE1) is true. It is also required that the #$ScriptedEventType not be a single cycle of some continuing process, since in that case the first scene would come both before and after the last scene.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed872).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed872,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed872,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
