x_comment(x_Training,"A specialization of #$Learning and #$ServiceEvent. Each instance of this collection is an event in which some #$Agent-PartiallyTangible receives instruction in certain skills (usually, but not always, from another #$Agent-PartiallyTangible). See also #$Education, #$Teaching, #$TrainingInFn, #$TrainingForFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed94a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed94a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed94a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed94a,9062).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed94a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed94a,[]).
x_comment('x_typeSoleFunction-AgnosticIntentional',"A type-level version of\n#$soleFunction. (#$typeSoleFunction-AgnosticIntentional\nAGTTYPE SITTYPE ROLE) means that in ordinary or normal circumstances,\ninstances of AGTTYPE have playing ROLE in situations of type SITTYPE\nas their only function in some immediate functional\ncontext. Like #$primaryFunction, the relation is\nagnostic as to whether the functionality derives from the intent of\nsome intentional designer: i.e., the relation is not a spec-pred of\n#$typeIntendedBehaviorCapable.  This is not to say that usage of\n#$typeSoleFunction-AgnosticIntentional implies that no design\nexists: the relation is simply agnostic as to whether a design exists\nor not.  For this reason #$typePrimaryFunction is deemed to be a\nspecialization.  In cases where SITTYPE is itself a #$FunctionalSystem\n(say, a kind of #$Cell or a kind of #$Organization in the\n#$FunctionalRoleAnalysisMt), SITTYPE itself specifies the functional\ncontext: otherwise, this context is taken to be specified by the\n#$Microtheory in which the assertion is made.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed94b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed94b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed94b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed94b,9063).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed94b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed94b,[]).
x_comment(x_conditionallyIndependent,"A predicate used for probability statements. (#$conditionallyIndependent PROPOSITION1 PROPOSITION2) means that, in the applicable #$Microtheory, PROPOSITION1 and PROPOSITION2 are conditionally independent of each other, that is, truth or falsehood of one does not increase or decrease the #$derivedProbability that the other is true.  They are, in a sense, irrelevant to each other.  In most microtheories, generally, the #$conditionallyIndependent statement means that when all we know in the #$Microtheory are its accessible #$domainAssumptions, then the truths of the two formulae are probabilistically independent.  See also #$conditionalProbability and #$conditionallyIndependentSentences.  This definition makes no presumption as to determinism versus nondeterminism, nor as to whether probability is only in the mind of some beholder.  The definition allows for any of several different systems of deriving the probability of one assertion from the probabilities of, or conditional probabilities relating, other assertions.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed94c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed94c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed94c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed94c,9080).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed94c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed94c,[]).
x_comment('x_Halo-CircleOfLight',"The collection of all halos of light.  A halo is a circle of light around, e.g., a #$AstronomicalBody.  This is a different concept than symbolic halo, which the constant #$Halo-Symbolic refers to.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed94d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed94d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
