x_comment(x_ClearlyDefinedEntireExternalSurface,"The collection of well-defined surfaces that surround certain spatial objects and which make up the boundary between being outside and inside of the object.  Most discrete objects such as #$Animal or #$PhysicalDevice have a #$ClearlyDefinedEntireExternalSurface as a #$surfaceParts.  If an object is hollow, such as a #$CavityOrContainer then it has an inside surface but it may or may not have a #$ClearlyDefinedEntireExternalSurface.  An example of a #$CavityOrContainer which does have a #$ClearlyDefinedEntireExternalSurface is a #$House-Modern.  Some objects, such as a #$Cave or a #$Ditch are #$CavityOrContainers that do not have a #$ClearlyDefinedEntireExternalSurface.  If one is in a cave digging into the inside surface of a cave wall, one never knows precisely when one has passed through to the other ``external'' side of the cave's wall.  This is not to say that such objects do not have some definable external surface.  Rather, whatever such surface they may be asserted as having, it is not something which the contextually relevant average person would agree on, know about, or find obviously true.  A #$Hill also does not have a #$ClearlyDefinedEntireExternalSurface since the external surface of a hill is not a #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded.  ") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed998).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed998,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed998,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed998,9252).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed998,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed998,[]).
x_comment(x_significantColorOfObject,"The formula (#$significantColorOfObject OBJCOL) means that the #$PartiallyTangible OBJ has a significant amount of the #$Color COL.  The color may be signinficant because it covers over some minimum percent of the surface area of OBJ (as yet undecided) or because it is symbolically important.  For example, the flag of #$RepublicOfSouthAfrica is Green, Red, Blue, Black, White, and Yellow.  Each of these colors is a #$significantColorOfObject of such a flag, even though the yellow covers less than 5% of the flag's surface.  If one refers to an object as being a set of colors, each color is related to the object via#$significantColorOfObject.  The more specialized predicate, #$mainColorOfObject should be used if the object could be referred to as being that color without mentioning any other color.  If we had a shirt that was green with a small, white insignia on the pocket, we WOULD, in this case, say that the #$mainColorOfObject, this shirt, is green.  The white would not be a #$significantColorOfObject.  See also #$objectHasColor and #$uniformColorOfObject.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed999).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed999,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed999,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed999,9253).
