x_comment(x_SharedNoteOnDocumentationExternalToTheKB,"#$SharedNoteOnDocumentationExternalToTheKB is a collection of #$SharedNotes which serve as pointers to documentation which is outside of the KB.  There is currently (Sept 99) no convention for how such pointers should be specified.  Thus until further use and analysis indicates otherwise it is suggested that one do this by studying other #$comment assertions on other elements of #$SharedNoteOnDocumentationExternalToTheKB. Although typical elements of #$SharedNoteOnDocumentationExternalToTheKB will point to text files (via a pathname) on the unix file system at #$Cycorp, one could imagine such that such elements might point to gif images, world wide web sites, sound files, threads of email conversations etc.. An example: #$NLAssertionsForQueryAugmentation is an element of #$SharedNoteOnDocumentationExternalToTheKB.  Its #$comment points to a text file about making NL assertions for the #$WebSearchEnhancementProject. Eventually, the interface can be modified to allow hotlinking between KB external objects (such as text files) and KB content.  In the meantime, #$SharedNoteOnDocumentationExternalToTheKB can serve to collect arbitrarily heterogeneous cases of desired linkups between KB content and information external to the KB.   Here are some motivating use cases: Suppose there is a lengthy FAQ document external to the KB which is important to use if one is making assertions using an assortment of constants such as #$acronymString, #$abbreviationString-PN, or #$nameString.  How is a neophyte going to even *know* about the existence of such an FAQ document?  How is an experienced user going to remember the pathname of this FAQ on a filesystem that frequently undergoes reorganizations?  How can someone who knows that information in the FAQ is highly relevant to a predicate that they want to use, yet they can't locate that precious 6 month old email message?  In all these cases, an element of #$SharedNoteOnDocumentationExternalToTheKB could easily point the user from the constant under consideration to what they need to know.  The predicate #$sharedNotes may be used to do the linking between the constant in question and the element of #$SharedNoteOnDocumentationExternalToTheKB.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed9e5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed9e5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed9e5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed9e5,9400).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed9e5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed9e5,[]).
x_comment('x_NullSpaceRegion-2D',"A specialization of #$NullSpaceRegion (q.v.) and #$TwoDimensionalThing.  #$NullSpaceRegion-2D is the collection of two-dimensional \"null spatial regions\".  There is at most one known instance of #$NullSpaceRegion-2D with respect to any context in which some #$Surfaces (q.v.) are known to have locations, either absolutely or relatively (see #$LocationalPredicate and its immediate specializations).\n<p>\nBy definition, a 2-D null space region is disconnected from (see #$disconnectedSpaceRegions) every 2-D homogeneous space region (i.e. #$Surface), and is the #$spaceRegionComplement of the 2-D \"spatial universe\" (see #$UniversalSpaceRegion-2D).\n<p>\nThe introduction of 2-D null regions into #$RegionConnectionCalculus8Theory helps to allow the functions #$SpaceRegionComplementFn, #$SpaceRegionProductFn, and #$SpaceRegionDifferenceFn to be defined as #$TotalFunctions.  For example, #$SpaceRegionComplementFn can then be defined on instances of #$UniversalSpaceRegion-2D (q.v.), and #$SpaceRegionProductFn can be defined for pairs of two-dimensional regions that are #$discreteSpaceRegions.  (Also see the comment on #$NullSpaceRegion.)\n<p>\nSee also #$NullSpaceRegion-1D and #$NullSpaceRegion-3D.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed9e6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed9e6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed9e6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ed9e6,9401).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ed9e6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ed9e6,[]).
x_comment(x_requiredTemplateForType,"(#$requiredTemplateForType TEMPLATE TYPE) means that when #$FormulaTemplate TEMPLATE is asserted to be an instance of #$FormulaTemplateType (or subtopic) TYPE, that template must be filled out by the user. For example, (#$requiredTemplateForType #$SourceTitleTemplate #$TKBTemplate-Source-JournalForm) means that in the subtopic for journal articles that are TKB sources, 'title' a required field. This assertion should be used for any template that we wish to force a fact enterer to complete. It is recommended that required templates be sorted (via #$higherPriorityTemplateForType) below the #$focalTermIntroduction template, and above all others.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ed9e7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ed9e7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ed9e7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
