xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eda1d,[]).
x_comment(x_contraryIntervalTypes,"(#$contraryIntervalTypes TYPE-1 TYPE-2) says that the #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityTypes TYPE-1 and TYPE-2 are contrary, in that they cannot both hold of the same object along the same scale to the same degree at the same time.  So, for example, generosity and miserliness are contrary (i.e., (#$contraryIntervalTypes #$Generosity #$Miserliness)): Suppose (#$isa DONATION-001 #$CharitableDonation), and (#$performedBy DONATION-001 PERSON001).  It cannot be the case that (#$and (exhibitsQualityDuringPerformanceOfAction PERSON LEVEL-OF-GENEROSITY DONATION-001) (exhibitsQualityDuringPerformanceOfAction PERSON LEVEL-OF-MISERLINESS DONATION-001)), so long as LEVEL-OF-GENEROSITY and LEVEL-OF-MISERLINESS are of the same degree on the same scale.  For example, the above conjunction would be false if LEVEL-OF-GENEROSITY were (#$HighAmountFn #$Generosity) and LEVEL-OF-MISERLINESS were (#$HighAmountFn #$Miserliness).  However, the above *could* be true if the two differ in degree (e.g., (#$VeryHighAmountFn #$Generosity) versus (#$VeryLowAmountFn #$Miserliness)) or if they appear on different scales (e.g., (#$RelativeGenericValueFn #$degreeOfGenerosity #$UnitedStatesPerson #$veryHighAmountOf) versus (#$RelativeGenericValueFn #$degreeOfMiserliness ObscenelyWealthyPerson #$veryHighAmountOf)).  Notably, contrary properties are capable of combining to form a larger scale that ranges from one #$scaleEndPoints of an interval-type to a #$scaleEndPoints of its contrary interval type (e.g., a scale ranging from the most generous to the most selfish); see #$scaleDefinedByContraries.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eda1e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eda1e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eda1e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eda1e,9506).
