x_comment(x_unboundPredicateRules,"(#$unboundPredicateRules PREDICATE) means that when the inference engine, in the course of backchaining, is attempting to prove a literal of the form (PREDICATE ARG1 ... ARGN) or the negation of such a literal, rules (in accessible microtheries) containing literals with unbound predicates will be available.  For example, the consequent of the rule (#$implies (#$and (#$seriesOrderedByInverse ?SER ?PRED) (#$seriesLength ?SER ?L) (#$greaterThanOrEqualTo ?L ?N) (#$greaterThan ?N ?M)) (?PRED (#$NthInSeriesFn ?SER ?N) (#$NthInSeriesFn ?SER ?M))) has an unbound predicate, the variable ?PRED.  Given that the KB contains the assertion (#$unboundPredicateRules #$caudalTo), that rule can be used in backchaining to prove, for instance, (#$caudalTo (#$NthInSeriesFn (#$The #$Vertebrae) 20) (#$NthInSeriesFn (#$The #$Vertebrae) 6)).") :- x_cid(a65b16505edaa1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505edaa1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505edaa1,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
