x_comment(x_IntervalRangeFn,"A binary #$ScalarDenotingFunction that takes two comparable scalar intervals to the (usually proper, usually continuous) interval between and including them.\n<p>\nWhere <code>SCALAR1</code> and <code>SCALAR2</code> are #$ScalarIntervals (q.v.) such that the maximum of the latter is #$greaterThanOrEqualTo the minimum of the former, <code>(#$IntervalRangeFn SCALAR1 SCALAR2)</code> is a #$ScalarInterval <code>RANGE</code> whose minimum (see #$minQuantValue) is the same as that of <code>SCALAR1</code> and whose maximum (see #$maxQuantValue) is the same as that of <code>SCALAR2</code>.  If both <code>SCALAR1</code> and <code>SCALAR2</code> are #$Integers (or proper integer-extents), then <code>RANGE</code> is the <i>contiguous</i> #$IntegerExtent (q.v.) that ranges from the minimum of the former to the maximum of the latter.  Otherwise (e.g. if <code>SCALAR1</code> and <code>SCALAR2</code> are #$RealNumbers or #$Quantity[ies]), then <code>RANGE</code> is the <i>continuous</i> interval that ranges from the minimum of the former to the maximum of the latter.\n<p>\nNote that <code>RANGE</code> will be a #$ScalarProperInterval (q.v.) except when <code>SCALAR1</code> #$equals <code>SCALAR2</code> and is itself a #$ScalarPointValue (q.v.).  When both of its arguments are the same, #$IntervalRangeFn behaves like an identity function: <code>(#$IntervalRangeFn SCALAR SCALAR) = SCALAR</code> for any scalar interval <code>SCALAR</code>.  When its arguments are #$NumericIntervals that are <i>not</i> integer-extents, #$IntervalRangeFn behaves exactly like #$Unity (q.v.).\n<p>\nIn practice, the two arguments to #$IntervalRangeFn will typically be either non-overlapping #$NonNumericScalarQuantity[ies] (q.v.), distinct point-value #$Quantity[ies], or particular numbers.\n<p>\nSome examples. (#$IntervalRangeFn #$Cold #$Cool) is #$CoolToCold: the temperature-range from #$Cold to #$Cool.  (#$IntervalRangeFn (#$Mile 5) (#$Mile 7)) is equal to (#$Mile 5 7): the distance-range of between 5 an 7 miles inclusive.  (#$IntervalRangeFn 1 10) is the #$IntegerExtent that comprises the integers from 1 to 10 inclusive. (#$IntervalRangeFn 1.0 10.0) is equal to (#$Unity 1.0 10.0): the #$ContinuousIntervalOnNumberLine that comprises the reals from 1.0 to 10.0 inclusive.\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding predicate #$intervalRange.") :- x_cid(a65b16505edaa6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505edaa6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505edaa6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
