x_comment(x_similarWRTTypeViaBinRel,"This predicate is used to make the similarity between two things more explicit.\n\n  (#$similarWRTTypeViaBinRel THING1 THING2 COL BINPRED) means that\n\nTHING1 and THING2 both bear the relation BINPRED to similar instances\n\nof COL, i.e., if there is an instance of COL, COLINS1 such that\n\n(BINPRED THING1 COLINS1) then either (BINPRED THING2 COLINS1) or\n\nthere is some similar instance of COL, COLINS2, such that (BINPRED\n\n                              THING2\n\n                              COLINS2).  \n\n For instance, (#$similarWRTTypeViaBinRel #$BillClinton\n\n#$GeorgeWBush #$Automobile #$likesObject) means that Clinton and Bush have similar tastes in automobiles.  Note\n\nthat (#$similarWRTTypeViaBinRel THING1 THING2 PRED COL) does not necessarily imply that (#$similarTo THING1 THING2), however, we might expect that claims of similarity between THING1 and THING2 be accompanied by clarification of the respects in which THING1 and THING2 are similar.  Note that this predicate, along with #$similarTo, is intended as a very general starting place for the development of improved vocabulary to discuss similarity in Cyc.  Use or introduce a specification, e.g. #$similarWRTPartType, (or see #$resemblesInQuantity or #$identicalInQuantity) when possible or feasible.") :- x_cid(a65b16505edbbf).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505edbbf,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505edbbf,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
