x_comment('x_intendedOrientation-DeviceType',"An instance of #$BinaryPredicate.\n#$intendedOrientation-DeviceType describes the intended orientation,\nwith respect to the object acted on, of a #$PhysicalDevice being used\naccording to its #$intendedPrimaryFunction. The orientation is\nrequired in order for the device to be causally efficacious in\nfulfilling the primary function. The orientation is specified in terms\nof a spatial relation that a specified type of part of the device must\nbear to a specified sort of #$situationConstituents in the device's\nintended use.\n<p>\nIn particular, intendedOrientation-DeviceType (DEVICE-TYPE PART-TYPE\nROLE RELATION) means that during an instance of the intended primary\nfunction of a device of type DEVICE-TYPE, the intended orientation of\nthe device consists in one or more of its physical parts of type\nPART-TYPE bearing the relation RELATION to one or more of the things\nthat play the role ROLE in the event.  For example,\n(intendedOrientation-DeviceType WritingPen PenTip objectMarked touchesDirectly)\nmeans that during any instance of MarkingOnASurfaceWithAPen, a #$PenTip of\nthe #$WritingPen that is the #$deviceUsed should bear\nthe #$touches-Directly relation to the #$objectMarked.\n<p>\nNote that the intended orientation need not obtain throughout all of\nthe intended-use event. Rather it must obtain throughout all of each\nof a certain type of subevent of the intended-use event. For\nexample, although the pen tip need not touch the writing surface during\nall of a given writing event (e.g., because the writer might lift the\npen to dot an \"i\"), the pen tip does need to touch the writing\nsurface throughout all of each of the writing event's subevents that\nare of type #$MarkingOnASurfaceWithAPen-Uninterrupted.") :- x_cid(a65b16505edbe5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505edbe5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505edbe5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505edbe5,10471).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505edbe5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505edbe5,[]).
x_comment(x_scalarDimensionalRank,"A binary predicate that is used to associate a given #$ScalarInterval with a rank (represented by an integer), as determined by the complexity of the dimensional analysis (see #$unitTypeDimensionalAnalysis) relevant to that scalar.  \n<p>\n<code>(#$scalarDimensionalRank SCALAR RANK)</code> means that <code>SCALAR</code> has rank <code>RANK</code>, where <code>RANK</code> is determined as follows.  If <code>SCALAR</code> is a #$NumericInterval (q.v.) then <code>RANK</code> is 0.  If <code>SCALAR</code> is a #$MeasurableQuantity measurable in units of a #$FundamentalUnitType (q.v.) then <code>RANK</code> is 1.  If <code>SCALAR</code> is a quantity measurable in units of a derived unit-type whose dimensional analysis is:\n<pre>\n  (#$TheSet\n    (#$TheList UNITTYPE-1 EXPONENT-1)\n    (#$TheList UNITTYPE-2 EXPONENT-2)\n    ...\n    (#$TheList UNITTYPE-n EXPONENT-n))\n</pre>\n<p>\nthen <code>RANK</code> is the sum of the absolute values of all of the <code>EXPONENT-i (1 <= i <= n)</code>.  Otherwise (e.g. if <code>SCALAR</code> is a #$NonNumericScalarQuantity), it is not associated with any rank.\n<p>\nFor example, the rank of (#$Meter 5) is 1, as #$Meter is a #$UnitOfDistance, which is a #$FundamentalUnitType.  And the rank of (#$MilesPerHour 25) is 2, as #$MilesPerHour is a #$UnitOfSpeed, which has the analysis:\n<pre>\n  (#$TheSet\n    (#$TheList #$UnitOfDistance 1)\n    (#$TheList #$UnitOfTime -1)) ;\n</pre>\n<p>\nand 1 + |-1| = 2.  Similarly, the volume (#$CubicFoot 18) has rank 3.") :- x_cid(a65b16505edbe6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505edbe6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505edbe6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505edbe6,10500).
