x_comment(x_FixedOrderCollection,"A #$CollectionType and the collection of all and only those #$Collections whose own instances are all of the same fixed \"order\", where order is understood as follows.  Let individuals (i.e. instances of #$Individual) be \"zeroth-order\" things, collections of (only) individuals \"first-order\" collections, ..., and collections of (only) <code>N</code>th-order things \"<code>(N</code>+1)th-order\" collections. More precisely, then, a #$Collection <code>COL</code> is a #$FixedOrderCollection if and only if it is an <code>N</code>th-order collection (for <code>N</code> >= 1).\n<p>\nInstances of #$FixedOrderCollection include #$Individual, #$FirstOrderCollection, #$SecondOrderCollection, #$ThirdOrderCollection, #$FourthOrderCollection, and all specializations of these.  Specializations of #$FixedOrderCollection include all of the preceding examples except #$Individual and its specializations.\n<p>\nAn example of a collection that is <i>not</i> fixed-order is #$AbstractThing, which is a varied-order collection since it contains both (zeroth-order) abstract individuals and (first-and-higher-order) collections.\n<p>\nNote that, although they are mutually disjoint, #$FixedOrderCollection and #$VariedOrderCollection (q.v.) do <i>not</i> constitute a partition of #$Collection: since the notion of order described above is not applicable to sets, #$Set-Mathematical (e.g.) and its specializations are neither fixed-order nor varied-order collections.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ee1d8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ee1d8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ee1d8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ee1d8,12364).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ee1d8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ee1d8,[]).
