x_comment('x_Nt-TheSuffix',"This is an unusual suffix in that it only attaches to inflected forms, rather than #$baseForm forms. Indeed, many linguists have analyzed it as a clitic instead of a suffix.  But the idea that this is a suffix rather than a clitic was argued for by Zwicky and Pullum (1983) in Language, on the following grounds: (A) \"n't\" is very picky about what it attaches to (only finite auxiliary verbs, not any verb that can precede \"not\", such as \"try\"); (B) there are arbitrary gaps in the paradigm (*amn't, *mayn't); (C) there are morphological idiosynracies (won't, shan't, can't); (D) there are semantic idiosynracies: mustn't means must(not) but can't means not(can).") :- x_cid(a65b16505ee238).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ee238,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ee238,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
