x_comment(x_regexStringMatch,"A variable-arity text-processing predicate used to indicate a match between a 'regular expression template' and a string of characters. (#$regexStringMatch REGEX STRING) means that the #$RegularExpression template (or 'regex') REGEX matches some substring within STRING. This is like the predicate #$substring, except that it allows for 'wildcard characters' and other regular expression features in the template. When used with three or more arguments, the argument after the second are bound to any strings that are captured by the regular expression.  For example, a query using the sentence:<p>\n<code>(#$regexStringMatch \"dogs(.*)cats(.*)\" \"dogs hate cats but love mice\" ?MATCH1 ?MATCH2)</code><p>\nreturns the following bindings:<p>\n<code>(((?MATCH1 . \" hate \") (?MATCH2 . \" but love mice\")))</code><p>\n\nNote that predicate currently supports only variables in the arg3+ argument position, and that arg1 and arg2 must be fully bound.<p>\n\nThe rules for what characters to use in the regex, and what constitutes a valid match, depend on the particular regular expression matcher being used. (Regular expressions are used for matching in the GREP, EGREP, AWK, VI, TCL, Emacs, PERL and PYTHON programs.) Some regex features are common to all such programs, including these: . [...] [^...] char ? * + ^ $ \\< \\> | (...) \\1,\\2,\\3,... Some other features vary. This predicate assumes that the PERL specification is used for regular expression matching. Briefly: The period matches 'any character'. Square brackets match a class of characters, those listed, possibly with ranges like A-Z or a-z or 1-8. Square brackets with a caret-character as the first included character match the class of characters other than those listed between the brackets. Backslash-character means the following special character is directly matched and is not treated as a special character. Caret matches the position of the start-of-line. Dollar-sign matches the position at the end-of-line. \\< matches any start of word. \\> matches any end-of-word. Parentheses group whatever they enclose. A character or group followed by a question-mark is optional; it matches zero or one occurence of that group or charater. A character or group followed by an asterisk matches any number of repetitions that group or character, including zero. A character or group followed by a plus-sign matches one or more repetitions of that group or character. A vertical bar | separates alternative matches -- either may succeed. \\1,\\2,\\3, etc. are backreference variables to which each matched group is assigned in order, and they will match later occurrences of the assigned group. For further details, see standard documentation for regular expressions or for PERL.  See also #$regexFileMatch.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ee30c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ee30c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ee30c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ee30c,12745).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ee30c,[]).
