x_comment(x_AbstractTheoreticalDestinationPoint,"The collection of all instances of #$EnduringThing-Localized that humans tend to treat as points, or \"immediate\" locations, i.e., as places where one might be used to answer questions such as \"where you are right now?\" or \"where can I meet you?\" in a way that assumes a high likelihood that someone familiar with the answer would know (pretty much) exactly where one is.  Accordingly, typical instances are places where, or objects around which, people might be expected to convene.  Thus typical instances are stationary objects or locations that 1) are easy to identify and to describe to others and, 2) are not so small or so large that it would be implausible or impractical to characterize them as meeting places or places where one might \"find\" a specific object or person (however, a particularly small object, such as a short line drawn in the sand or an unusual rock might count as an instance of this collection in situations where it has been designated as such (e.g., \"walk towards me, but stop once you get to <i>that blue pebble</i>.\").  Common examples include desks, parked cars, buildings, and landmarks.  Counterexamples include geopolitical entities (e.g., cities, counties, etc. are simply too large -- you can be located \"in\" them, but not located \"at\" them -- at least not in any sense where it would make sense to identify such a thing as a meeting place or an \"immediate\" location.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ee3a4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ee3a4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ee3a4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ee3a4,12910).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ee3a4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ee3a4,[]).
