x_comment(x_Centromere,"The collection of centromeres.  A centromere is a series of nucleotide pairs on a fully formed strand of chromatin (see #$ChromatinStrand) where the two constituent chromatids (see #$chromatids) of a duplicated chromosome (see #$DuplicatedChromosome) meet.  During Anaphase, kinetochores (see #$Kinetochore) attached to the centromeres become attached to spindle fibers which separate the chromatids, resulting in two chromosomes.  The collection #$Centromere is to be distinguished from the more general notion of the centromeric region (see #$centromericRegion), which, by convention, is a property of chromosomes, and not chromatids, which, by virtue of containing fully formed DNA molecules, do have centromeres.  Moreover, every chromosome has a single centromeric region, while a chromosome (specifically, a duplicated chromosome, by virtue of having two chromatids) may have two centromeres.  In the biological literature, \"centromere\" almost always refers to the unique centromeric region of a chromosome, but its sense \"slides\" to the more precise sense represented by #$Centromere when discussions turn to the details of cell division.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ee40a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ee40a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ee40a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ee40a,13026).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ee40a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ee40a,[]).
x_comment(x_centromericRegion,"<code>(#$centromericRegion CHROMOSOME REGION)</code> identifies <code>REGION</code> as the so-called \"primary constriction\" of <code>CHROMOSOME</code>, an instance of #$Chromosome.  The centromeric region consists of, in the case of a #$UnduplicatedChromosome, its centromere (see #$Centromere), but in the case of a #$DuplicatedChromosome, the mereological sum of the centromeres of its constituent chromatids (see #$chromatids).") :- x_cid(a65b16505ee40b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ee40b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
