x_comment(x_HaplonticLifeCycle,"The specialization of #$SexualLifeCycle that occurs in haplonts (see #$Haplont).  In this cycle, the predominant, multicellular form of the organism is haploid (see #$HaploidOrganism).  The diploid phase is limited to the zygote (see #$Zygote), which does not divide mitotically to produce a multicellular organism, but rather divides via #$Meiosis to produce #$HaploidCells sometimes called spores.  These spores divide mitotically to generate the mature haploid organism (see #$HaploidOrganism).  When sexually mature, the haploid phase can produce gametes (see #$Gamete); these can participate in fertilization to produce a #$Zygote.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ee857).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ee857,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ee857,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ee857,14185).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ee857,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ee857,[]).
x_comment(x_Haplont,"The collection of organisms that particpate in the #$HaplonticLifeCycle.  In this cycle, the predominant, multicellular form of the organism is haploid (see #$HaploidOrganism).  The diploid phase is limited to the zygote (see #$Zygote), which does not divide mitotically to produce a multicellular organism, but rather divides via #$Meiosis to produce #$HaploidCells sometimes called spores.  These spores divide mitotically to generate the mature haploid organism (see #$HaploidOrganism).  When sexually mature, the haploid phase can produce gametes (see #$Gamete); these can participate in fertilization to produce a #$Zygote.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ee858).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ee858,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ee858,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ee858,14186).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ee858,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ee858,[]).
x_comment(x_DiplonticLifeCycle,"The specialization of #$SexualLifeCycle that occurs in diplonts (see #$Diplont).   In this cycle, the predominant multicellular form of the organism is diploid (see #$DiploidOrganism).  Meiosis (see #$Meiosis) occurs in the sex organs of the mature diploid individual to produce the only haploid form of the organism, the gametes (see #$Gamete), which are single cells that do not divide mitotically.  Instead, two gametes (one male, one female) fuse in fertilization (see #$ReproductiveFertilizationEvent)  to form a diploid zygote (see #$Zygote) that then divides by mitosis to develop into the predominant, multicellular diploid form.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ee859).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ee859,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ee859,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ee859,14187).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ee859,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ee859,[]).
x_comment(x_Diplont,"The class of organisms whose #$SexualLifeCycle is the #$DiplonticLifeCycle.  In this cycle, the predominant multicellular form of the organism is diploid (see #$DiploidOrganism).  Meiosis (see #$Meiosis) occurs in the sex organs of the mature diploid individual to produce the only haploid form of the organism, the gametes (see #$Gamete), which are single cells that do not divide mitotically.  Instead, two gametes (one male, one female) fuse in fertilization (see #$ReproductiveFertilizationEvent)  to form a diploid zygote (see #$Zygote) that then divides by mitosis to develop into the predominant, multicellular diploid form.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ee85a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ee85a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ee85a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ee85a,14188).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ee85a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ee85a,[]).
x_comment(x_DiplobionticLifeCycle,"A specialization of #$SexualLifeCycle that is exhibited haplodiplontic organisms (see #$Haplodiplont).  This includes most plants, some algae and some fungi.  In this cycle, mitotic cell division (see #$MitoticPhaseOfCellCycle) occurs in both the haploid and the diploid phases of the cycle; thus, both haploid and diploid multicellular invididuals exist (see #$HaploidOrganism and #$DiploidOrganism).  This life cycle type is also called ''alternation of generations.''  The zygote (see #$Zygote) divides mitotically to develop into a diploid multicellular organism, also called a sporophyte (see #$Sporophyte).  When mature, meiosis (see #$Meiosis) occurs in the sex organs of this organism to produce haploid cells (see #$HaploidCell) called spores.  These spores divide mitotically to develop into a multicellular haploid organism called a gametophyte (see #$Gametophyte).  When this organism is mature, certain cells undergo mitosis to produce gametes (see #$Gamete).  Two gametes of opposite kinds (most commonly called male and female) fuse in fertilization (see #$ReproductiveFertilizationEvent) to form a diploid zygote, and the cycle repeats.<br>\n\nIn a particular species, the haploid and diploid forms may be similar in appearance and adaptation, or very different.  Typically the diploid sporophyte is larger and longer lived than the gametophyte, and in the case of vascular plants gametophytes are not free-living but are dependent on the sporophyte.  In some plants, such as most mosses and liverworts, the haploid gametophyte is larger and longer-lived.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ee85b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ee85b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ee85b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ee85b,14189).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ee85b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ee85b,[]).
x_comment(x_Haplodiplont,"An instance of #$EukaryoticOrganismTypeByLifeCycleType, this is the collection of organisms that participate in the diplobiontic life cycle (see #$DiplobionticLifeCycle), a life cycle typical of plants and some algae.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ee85c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ee85c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
