x_comment(x_IteratedEventScene,"Specializations of #$IteratedEventScene are event-types instances of which are events in which some event is repeated (in sequence, synchronously or haphazardly) at least twice, possibly indefinitely, and sometimes until a terminal condition (such as a goal event) is achieved.  By convention, the terminal state type is considered to be a sub-event type of the iterated event type, but it need not be the same type as the iterated event.  For example, mixing a cake batter may involve iterations of stirring the batter, and they are terminated when the batter reaches a certain consistency, but reaching a consistency is not itself a stirring.  Some terminal conditions of iterated event types are of the same kind as the iterated events -- for example, in a script that requires taking eight leaps, taking the 8th leap may be the terminal event, and it itself is a leap (which is also the iterated event type).") :- x_cid(a65b16505eeab3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eeab3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eeab3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eeab3,14843).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505eeab3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eeab3,[]).
x_comment(x_RoundOfCellDivision,"Each instance of #$RoundOfCellDivision consists of one or more #$CellDivision events which often, but don't necessarily, occur simultaneously.  What defines a set of divisions as being part of the same #$RoundOfCellDivision is that the parent cells in each division all share the same ancestor cell, from which they are descended via the same number of cell divisions.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eeab4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eeab4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
