x_comment(x_stepFollowsStepInCycleType,"A predicate used to characterize orderings of steps in cycles. <code>(#$stepFollowsStepInCycleType CYCLE LATER-STEP EARLIER-STEP)</code> means that in an instance of <code>CYCLE</code>, a specialization of #$Cycle-Situation, an instance of <code>LATER-STEP</code> will be \"the next step\" to occur after an instance of <code>EARLIER-STEP</code>.  What counts as \"the next step\" is a matter of granularity.  For example, in the (eukaryotic) cell cycle, the interphase step follows the M phase step which follows another iteration of the cycle, so that <code>(#$stepFollowsStepInCycleType #$TheEukaryoticCellCycle #$InterphaseOfEukaryoticCellCycle #$MitoticPhaseOfCellCycle)</code> and both <code>(#$stepFollowsStepInCycleType #$TheEukaryoticCellCycle #$MitoticPhaseOfCellCycle #$InterphaseOfEukaryoticCellCycle)</code> hold.  However, M phase consists of two phases, Mitosis and Cytokinesis, so that in addition, <code>(#$stepFollowsStepInCycleType #$InterphaseOfEukaryoticCellCycle  #$Mitosis)</code> and <code>(#$stepFollowsStepInCycleType #$Cytokinesis #$InterphaseOfEukaryoticCellCycle)</code> both hold.  However, it would does not hold that <code>(#$stepFollowsStepInCycleType #$Cytokinesis #$InterphaseOfEukaryoticCellCycle)</code> since, despite the fact that cytokinesis does indeed follow interphase <i>temporally</i> (e.g., <code>(#$startsAfterStartOfInSituationType #$TheEukaryoticCellCycle #$InterphaseOfEukaryoticCellCycle #$Cytokinesis)</code> undeniably holds), it is not \"next\" in the way that #$Mitosis and #$MitoticPhaseOfCellCycle are.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eed1a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eed1a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eed1a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eed1a,15565).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505eed1a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eed1a,[]).
x_comment(x_phasesAlternateInCycleType,"A predicate used to identify the two alternating \"top level\" stages in a cycle, for cycles that admit of such a description. <code>(#$phasesAlternateInCycleType CYCLE PHASE-A PHASE-B)</code> identify <code>PHASE-A</code> and <code>PHASE-B</code> as the alternating stages.  Typically, this means that <code>CYCLE</code> is a serial cycle that iterates through some class of event <code>EVENT</code> in which <code>PHASE-A</code> is followed by <code>PHASE-B</code> or vice versa.  For example, <code>(#$phasesAlternateInCycleType #$TheEukaryoticCellCycle #$InterphaseOfEukaryoticCellCycle #$MitoticPhaseOfCellCycle)</code> holds by virtue of #$TheEukaryoticCellCycle denoting the class of cycling events that iterate through a series of instances of #$EukaryoticCellCycle, which is defined as a class of events that begins with an instance of #$InterphaseOfEukaryoticCellCycle followed by (and ending with) an instance of #$MitoticPhaseOfCellCycle.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eed1b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eed1b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eed1b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eed1b,15566).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505eed1b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eed1b,[]).
x_comment('x_Cycle-Serial',"The collection of all instances of #$Cycle-Situation in which the iterated sequence of stages of stages comprising a single repetition of the #$Cycle-Situation is instantiated in temporal series; e.g. where a given #$Cycle-Situation SIT is constituted by situation types A, B, C, and D, if SIT is a #$Cycle-Serial, then an instantiation of A is followed temporally by an instantiation of B, followed by an instantiation of C, and so on.  #$Cycle-Serial instances contrast in this respect with instances of #$Cycle-Parallel, wherein instantiated stages happen concurrently.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eed1c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eed1c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eed1c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eed1c,15567).
