xc_kb_names(a65b16505eed30,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eed30,[]).
x_comment('x_Secretion-Bodily',"The collection of all #$NaturalTangibleStuff secreted by an organism's body.  There are a wide variety of bodily secretions, including #$Hormones, #$Mucus, #$Insulin, and #$Semen.  Secretions may be deposited either within the body (e.g., #$BileTheSecretion) or outside of it (e.g., #$Sweat). \n<p>\nNote: Secretions are different from #$ExcretionSubstance in that secretions are not necessarily waste products.  E.g., they may be a release of pheromones to attract a mate, a layer of sweat to cool off the organism, a layer of nectar to attract bees, etc.  Some subsets of #$Secretion-Bodily, such as #$Sweat, are also subsets of #$ExcretionSubstance (i.e., wastes).") :- x_cid(a65b16505eed31).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eed31,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eed31,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eed31,15596).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505eed31,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eed31,[]).
x_comment(x_biologicalObjectTypeSecretesType,"<code>(#$biologicalObjectTypeSecretesType BLO-TYPE SECRETION-TYPE)</code> means that instances of <code>BLO-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$BiologicalLivingObject) are sources of instances of <code>SECRETION-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible, and typically a specialization of #$Secretion-Bodily).  \n<p>\nFor example, all of the following are true:\n<p>\n(#$biologicalObjectTypeSecretesType #$IsletsOfLangerhans #$Insulin)<br>\n(#$biologicalObjectTypeSecretesType #$Thyroid #$ThyroidHormone) <br>\n(#$biologicalObjectTypeSecretesType #$TearDuct #$LacrimalFluid)\n<p>\nWhenever it is the case that (#$biologicalObjectTypeSecretesType BLO-TYPE SECRETION-TYPE), there is some type of #$SecretionEvent during which among the outputs created (see #$outputsCreated and #$outputsCreated-TypeType) are instances of SECRETION-TYPE, and during which instances of BLO-TYPE are the doers.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eed32).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eed32,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eed32,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eed32,15599).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505eed32,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eed32,[]).
x_comment(x_onlyBiologicalObjectTypeProducesType,"<code>(#$onlyBiologicalObjectTypeSecretesType BLO-TYPE PRODUCT-TYPE)</code> means that only instances of <code>BLO-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$BiologicalLivingObject) are sources (during instances of #$BiologicalProductionEvent) of instances of <code>PRODUCT-TYPE</code> (a specialization of #$PartiallyTangible).  \n<p>\nFor example, all of the following are true:\n<p>\n(#$onlyBiologicalObjectTypeSecretesType #$BananaTree (#$FruitFn #$BananaTree)) <br>\n(#$onlyBiologicalObjectTypeSecretesType #$Gonad #$Gamete)\n<p>\nWhenever it is the case that <code>(#$onlyBiologicalObjectTypeProducesType BLO-TYPE PRODUCT-TYPE)</code>, there is some type of production event (see #$BiologicalProductionEvent) in which among the outputs created (see #$outputsCreated and #$outputsCreated-TypeType) are instances of <code>PRODUCT-TYPE</code>, and during which only instances of <code>BLO-TYPE</code> are important participants.\n<p>\nIt is often the case that instances of <code>PRODUCT-TYPE</code> can be synthesized, and thus are sometimes produced by means other than a <i>biological</i> production event.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eed33).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eed33,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eed33,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
