x_comment(x_Biomineralization,"Instances are process in which the tissues of living things form mineral (thus crystalline) solids.  #$Biomineralization can be a natural and beneficial process, because hard solids can protect the organism (as in the shells of #$Mollusks or of #$CoralPolyps) or give it shape and support its tissues (as in the #$SkeletalSystems of #$Vertebrates).  Mineral solids can also form as a result of disease processes, such as with #$KidneyStones.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eee51).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eee51,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eee51,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eee51,15951).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505eee51,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eee51,[]).
x_comment(x_BoneMineralization,"Mineralization is the last step in the creation of bone extracellular matrix and it invovles the formation of hydroxyapatite nanocrsytals (see #$BoneMineral) within a collagenous framework.  During growth of vertebrates, the flat bones of the skull, mandibles, maxillae and clavicles are shaped first in non-cartilaginous connective tissue, while long bones are originally ''modeled'' in cartilage (see #$Cartilage).  Parts of bones which have already become mineralized are immediately subject to #$BoneRemodeling, in which old bone is dissolved and new bone is deposited.  During remodeling, collagenous #$Osteoid is deposited, which subsequently becomes hardened during #$BoneMineralization, thus completing the creation of new bone extracelllar matrix.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eee52).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eee52,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eee52,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eee52,15952).
