x_comment(x_nextInSequence,"(#$nextInSequence LIST THING) means that it seems natural to think that  a principle determines the sequence of elements N0...Nn in LIST, and that if that same principle were applied to determine the sequence in a larger list N0...Nn, Nn+1, then Nn+1 would be THING.  For example, the following are true:\n<p>\n<pre>\n(#$nextInSequence (#$TheList 1 2 3) 4)\n(#$nextInSequence (#$TheList #$G1Phase #$SPhase) #$G2Phase)\n</pre>\n<p>\nNote that the second argument position of #$nextInSequence is not functional.  For example, both of the following are true:\n<p>\n<pre>\n(#$nextInSequence (#$TheList 2 4) 8)\n(#$nextInSequence (#$TheList 2 4) 6)\n</pre>\n<p>\nThese are both true because the principle behind the sequence 2,4 might either be \"to generate any element after the first, multiply its predecessor by 2\"  (i.e., apply (#$Lambda (?PREDECESSOR) (#$TimesFn ?PREDECESSOR 2))) or \"to generate any element after the first, add 2 to its predecessor\" (i.e., apply (#$Lambda (?PREDECESSOR) (#$PlusFn ?PREDECESSOR 2))).") :- x_cid(a65b16505eeeb7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eeeb7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eeeb7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eeeb7,16059).
