x_comment(x_PeptideMoleculeTypeBySourceGene,"A type-level collection. Each instance is a collection PEPTIDE-MOL-FAMILY of #$PeptideMolecules characterized by the fact that all of its instances are molecules derived by transcribing (see #$Transcription-DNAToRNA) and optionally translating (see #$Translation-RNAToProtein) a segment of DNA (an instance of #$Gene-HereditaryUnit) which is a physical instantiation (see #$instantiationOfGene-GIS) of an instance of some #$GeneTypeBySpeciesAndProductFamily. Note that instances of each PEPTIDE-MOL-FAMILY are actual #$PeptideMolecules, but they are not necessarily all identical.  Sources of variation are as follows: a) Small differences in the genetic sequences of different DNA segments that all belong the same species of organism and code for the same product family can result in slightly different amino acid sequences being produced, which are still functionally more or less the same. Single Nucleotide Polymorphisms are one example of this type of variation. b) Alternative splicing occurs frequently.  This means that the same segment of DNA can produce RNA that is spliced (see #$RNASplicingProcess) in different ways to produce messenger RNAs that code for very different proteins.  However, each of these splice variants shares large subregions containing identical amino acid sequences.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eef05).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eef05,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eef05,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eef05,16154).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505eef05,[]).
