x_comment(x_predicateIsPredicateCompositionOf,"<code>(#$predicateIsPredicateCompositionOf \n COMP-PRED PRED-1 PRED-2)</code> means that the binary predicate <code>COMP-PRED</code> is the composition of the binary predicates <code>PRED-1</code> and <code>PRED-2</code>. If <code>(#$predicateIsPredicateCompositionOf \n COMP-PRED PRED-1 PRED-2)</code>, then <code>(COMP-PRED X Z)</code> if and only if there is something, <code>Y</code>, such that <code>(PRED-1 X Y)</code> and <code>(PRED-2 Y Z)</code>. \n<p>\nSuppose, for example, that (#$predicateIsPredicateCompositionOf #$mothersName #$mother #$nameString). Then it follows from (#$mother #$GeorgeWBush #$BarbaraBush ) and (#$nameString #$BarbaraBush \"Barbara Bush\") that (mothersName\n#$GeorgeWBush \"Barbara Bush\"). The function corresponding to this\npredicate is #$BinaryCompositionPredicateFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ef1e9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ef1e9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ef1e9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
