x_comment('x_criticalPoint-TemperatureAtPressure',"<code>(#$criticalPoint-TemperatureAtPressure OBJ TEMP PRESSURE)</code> means that the instance #$PartiallyTangible will reach the equilibrium between gas and liquid when its temperature (#$temperatureOfObject) and ambient pressure (see #$ambientPressure) converge at <code>TEMP</code> and <code>PRESSURE</code>, respectively, all other variables held constant.  <code>TEMP</code> is the so-called \"critical temperature\" above which point <code>OBJ</code>, if in a gaseous state, cannot be liquified, regarless of the pressure.  <code>PRESSURE</code> is similarly the critical pressure, above which point <code>OBJ</code>, if in liquid form, cannot be transition into a gaseous state, regardless of the temperature.  At any point above the critical point, <code>OBJ</code> will be in a plasma state.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ef314).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ef314,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ef314,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ef314,17254).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ef314,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ef314,[]).
x_comment('x_dewPoint-TemperatureAtPressure',"<code>(#$dewPoint-TemperatureAtPressure OBJ TEMP PRESSURE)</code> means that the instance #$PartiallyTangible will, if in a gaseous state, form its first droplet of dew when its temperature (#$temperatureOfObject) and ambient pressure (see #$ambientPressure) converge at <code>TEMP</code> and <code>PRESSURE</code>, respectively, all other variables held constant. For chemically pure substances, #$dewPoint-TemperatureAtPressure is identical to #$boilingPoint-TemperatureAtPressure (and hence to #$bubblePoint-TemperatureAtPressure).  Dew point is useful to distinguish from boiling point when studying substances that are mixtures of other substances with different boiling points. In such circumstances, it used in conjunction with bubble point to calculate a multi-phase \"envelope\" in which a substance has both gas and liquid present.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ef315).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ef315,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ef315,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ef315,17255).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505ef315,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505ef315,[]).
x_comment('x_bubblePoint-TemperatureAtPressure',"<code>(#$bubblePoint-TemperatureAtPressure OBJ TEMP PRESSURE)</code> means that the instance #$PartiallyTangible will form its first bubble of gas when its temperature (#$temperatureOfObject) and ambient pressure (see #$ambientPressure) converge at <code>TEMP</code> and <code>PRESSURE</code>, respectively, all other variables held constant. For chemically pure substances, #$bubblePoint-TemperatureAtPressure is identical to #$boilingPoint-TemperatureAtPressure.  bubble point is useful to distinguish from boiling point when studying substances that are mixtures of other substances with different boiling points. In such circumstances, it used in conjunction with dew point (see #$dewPoint-TemperatureAtPressure) to calculate a multi-phase \"envelope\" in which a substance has both gas and liquid present.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ef316).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ef316,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
