x_comment(x_mostGeneralCoveringOfEventType,"A specialization of #$coveringOfEventType, <code>(#$mostGeneralCoveringOfEventType EVT-TYPE LIST-OF-EVT-TYPES)</code> that adds the additional restriction that the breakdown of <code>EVT-TYPE</code> via the list of event-types <code>LIST-OF-EVT-TYPES</code> is, in the current context, the most general, accepted way of decomposing <code>EVT-TYPE</code>.  Specifically, if #$coveringOfEventType holds, not only of <code>EVT-TYPE</code> and <code>LIST-OF-EVT-TYPES</code> but of <code>EVT-TYPE</code> and a <i>different</i> list <code>ALTERANTIVE-LIST</code>, then it must be the case that #$coveringOfEventType holds of at least one element of <code>LIST-OF-EVT-TYPES</code> and some sub-list of <code>ALTERNATIVE-LIST</code>.  For example, in the context of American sports, <code>(#$coveringOfEventType #$NFLSeason (#$TheList #$NFLPreSeason #$NFLRegularSeason #$NFLPostSeason))</code> and <code>(#$coveringOfEventType #$NFLSeason (#$TheList #$NFLPreSeason #$NFLRegularSeason #$NFLPostSeason #$SuperBowlGame))</code> are both true (both are complete decompositions of #$NFLSeason in correct temporal order); however, #$mostGeneralCoveringOfEventType only holds of #$NFLSeason and the former list, not the latter, as the latter replaces the more general #$NFLPostSeason with its decomposition into #$NFLPostSeason and #$SuperBowlGame.\n<p>\nNote the additional requirement that #$mostGeneralCoveringOfEventType hold of an event-type and the most general decomposition for which there is consensus in the current context.  This notion of consensus is intended to impose a \"naturalness\" requirement on the decomposition, so as to block arbitary decompositions into list of event-types that aren't particularly meaningful or salient in the relevant context. For example, one could introduce into the reified collection hierarchy an arbitrary collection, #$NFLPreseasonFollowedByNFLRegularSeason, which decomposes into #$NFLPreseason and #$NFLRegularSeason, and use it as a counterexample to the above claim that the most general decomposition of #$NFLSeason is the one that divides it into the three event-types.  However, in the context of American football, there is no generally recognized event-type that the constant #$NFLPreseasonFollowedByNFLRegularSeason denotes (despite the fact that it is a metaphysically \"real\" event-type) -- in the context in question, the world just isn't \"carved up\" that way.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ef387).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ef387,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505ef387,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505ef387,17376).
