x_comment(x_functionTypePredictsPairBasedOnInitialValues,"<code>(#$functionTypePredictsPairBasedOnInitialValues FUNC-TYPE XLIST YLIST X Y)</code> means that a function of type <code>FUNC-TYPE</code> predicts the pair <code><X, Y></code> given the set of pairs that would be created by associating the first member of <code>XLIST</code> (call it \"<code>X1</code>\") with the first member of <code>YLIST</code> (call it \"<code>Y1</code>\") to produce the pair <code><X1, Y1></code>, and associating the second member of <code>XLIST</code> (call it \"<code>X2</code>\") with the first member of <code>YLIST</code> (call it \"<code>Y2</code>\") to produce the pair <code><X2, Y2></code>, and so on. \n<p>\nThis predicate is not functional in general. It behaves like a functional predicate in both the arg4 and arg5 positions when the instance of the function type referenced in the arg1 position is 1-1.  Similarly, it behaves like a functional predicate in the arg4 position when the instance of the function type referenced in the arg1 position is 1-many, and it behaves like a functional predicate in the arg5 position when the instance of the function type referenced in the arg1 position is many-1.\n<p>\nThis predicate is agnostic as to whether it is doing interpolation or extrapolation.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ef4ba).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ef4ba,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
