x_comment(x_BufferOutOfBoundsWrite,"A specialization of #$ComputerProcessRunning.  Each instance of #$BufferOutOfBoundsWrite is an event in which data is input to a running process that pushes commands into the execution stack of the computer on which the program is running.  As a consequence, the program may crash, or the the return address may be overwritten so that it points back into the buffer.  Buffer overflows involve supplying the program a data string that is too large for the the buffer of the program to handle, via a function in the program that does not perform bounds checking. In the standard C library there are a number of functions that copy or append strings without bounds checking: strcat(), strcpy(), sprintf(), vsprintf(), gets(). See #$VulnerableToBufferOverflow,  #$BufferOverflowFault-ProgramCrashing, and #$BufferOverflowFault-CommandExecution.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ef77d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505ef77d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
