x_comment(x_AbsoluteQuantityConversionFn,"<code>(#$AbsoluteQuantityConversionFn UNIT QUANTITY)</code> converts the absolute <code>QUANTITY</code> into an equivalent absolute quantity expressed using <code>UNIT</code> as the unit of measure. For example, (#$AbsoluteQuantityConversionFn #$Kelvin (#$DegreeCelsius -273.15)) denotes the absolute quantity (#$Kelvin 0).  Contrast this with the relative qualtity conversion function, #$QuantityConversionFn. (#$QuantityConversionFn #$Kelvin (#$DegreeCelsius -273.15) denotes (#$Kelvin -273.15).  For units on scales that do not involve an offset, both <code>(#$AbsoluteQuantityConversionFn UNIT QUANTITY)</code> and <code>(#$QuantityConversionFn UNIT QUANTITY)</code> denote the same thing.") :- x_cid(a65b16505ef7b5).
