x_comment(x_bodilyOccursIn,"An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$eventOccursAt and #$nonDeliberateActors (qq.v.).  <code>(#$bodilyOccursIn EVENT ORGANISM)</code> means that the physical event <code>EVENT</code> occurs at or within the body of <code>ORGANISM</code>, an instance of #$Organism-Whole.  As such, #$bodilyOccursIn in is similar to and has overlap with #$bodilyDoer, the difference being an issue of the granularity of the event in question, where the granularity determines whether it makes sense to say that the organism is meaningfully the doer (#$doneBy) of the event.  At the \"coarsest\" end of the spectrum, where the entire body of <code>ORGANISM</code> is the doer of <code>EVENT</code> (e.g., #$Sleeping, #$HavingASeizure), <code>(#$bodilyDoer EVENT ORGANISM)</code> holds but <code>(#$bodilyOccursIn EVENT ORGANISM)</code> does not.  At the other end of the spectrum, from the level of cellular processes, down to chemical interactions that comprise them (e.g., #$Mitosis, #$CDKActivity (when it occurs in a body)), <code>(#$bodilyOccursIn EVENT ORGANISM)</code> holds but <code>(#$bodilyDoer EVENT ORGANISM)</code> does not.  In the middle are events, such as heart attacks, suffering from an infection, and event mass-events comprised of individual cell-level events in sufficiently massive quantities (e.g., #$Sweating), where both relations hold.") :- x_cid(a65b16505efbb5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505efbb5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
