x_comment('x_Population-Ecological',"The collection of naturally-occurring groups of organisms, such that each group consists of members of a single species, and all group members live more or less in the same location at a given time.  This notion of population is of importance to population geneticists and those who study the dynamics of individual naturally occurring populations, i.e. age and gender distributions, age at reproduction, fecundity, etc. Animal populations may be migratory like some birds and butterflies. Plant populations travel less, but populations of waterborne plants like algae may move great distances as water flows. Populations of different species will differ in their size and in the range that they cover, depending on the typical behaviors or habits of the particular type of organism.  Members of a #$Population-Ecological have at least the possibility, modulo gender compatibility and maturity, of interbreeding.  Physical barriers to breeding keep populations separate; for example, a river separating populations of mice.") :- x_cid(a65b16505efbf7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505efbf7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505efbf7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505efbf7,19782).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505efbf7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505efbf7,[]).
x_comment(x_PopulationOfBLOs,"The collection of groups of #$BiologicalLivingObjects, such that each group consists of members of a single species, and all group members live in the same location and interact to breed (if they do so), to communicate (e.g., via chemical signaling or language), and to compete for nutrients and space.  This collection includes naturally occurring populations as well as artificial ones such as cell cultures or ant farms. ") :- x_cid(a65b16505efbf8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505efbf8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505efbf8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
