x_comment(x_eventFocalLocation,"<code>(#$eventFocalLocation EVENT LOCATION)</code> means that the event <code>EVENT</code> is focally located at <code>LOCATION</code>, an instance of #$EnduringThing-Localized.  #$eventFocalLocation is more restrictive than its generalization, #$eventOccursAt, which applies to all instances of #$EnduringThing-Localized that spatially subsume every object to which it applies.  I.e., given a dancing-on-the-table event <code>DancingOnTheTable-13926</code> that takes place on a table <code>Table-43</code>, #$eventOccursAt will not only hold of <code>DancingOnTheTable-13926</code> and <code>Table-43</code>, it will hold of <code>DancingOnTheTable-13926</code> and the room in which <code>Table-43</code> resides, the floor of the building where the room is, the building itself, the city block of the building, the city, the country of the city, the planet Earth, and the entire physical universe.  However, #$eventFocalLocation holds only of <code>DancingOnTheTable-13926</code> and <code>Table-43</code>.  This is not to say that #$eventFocalLocation is functional or single-entry in its second argument place; depending on the granularity of the context, it might be that the #$eventFocalLocation of an event might include a range of locations.  For example, the operations of a land rover on Mars might, in the context of naive discussions about what's newsworthy, be such that the #$eventFocalLocation is the surface of Mars (or Mars itself), while in the context of NASA operations tracking the rover, the #$eventFocalLocation might be particular regions on the surface of Mars.  But in neither context is it likely that the solar system would count as the #$eventFocalLocation, even though it does fall under the extent of #$eventOccursAt.") :- x_cid(a65b16505efcd0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505efcd0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505efcd0,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
