x_comment(x_Bandsaw,"A type of #$Saw.  Instances of #$Bandsaw have a blade which is a continuous loop of thin metal with teeth along one narrow edge.  The thickness of the blade, its width, and the configuration of the teeth can be adjusted to optimize it for cutting various materials.  Various configurations exist.  Large metal-cutting bandsaws often keep the work piece stationary, and automatically lower the moving blade through the metal to cut it, while the area of the cut is bathed in coolant.  Smaller wood-cutting and meat-cutting bandsaws often have a horizontal cutting table and a vertically-oriented blade that is pulled down through an opening in the table by wheels above and below the table.  The operator manually moves the work piece past the blade.   Large bandsaws in sawmills may have the blade at various orientations, and often automatically move the timber to be cut through the blade, either via gravity or with an active feed system. ") :- x_cid(a65b16505efd6c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505efd6c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505efd6c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505efd6c,20173).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505efd6c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505efd6c,[]).
x_comment(x_BandsawBlade,"The collection of blades for #$Bandsaws.   Each is a continuous loop formed from a strip of steel which is much thinner than it is wide, with cutting teeth along one narrow edge.  Blades can be optimized for cutting wood, metal or meat.  Small bandsaws may use a blade that has a perimeter of a few feet; large ones may need blades with a perimeter of several yards.") :- x_cid(a65b16505efd6d).
