x_comment(x_ElectromagneticRadiationAbsorption,"A specialization of #$AbsorptionEvent.  Instances are events in which some quantity #$ElectromagneticRadiation enters some #$PartiallyTangible thing.  When a quantity of energy - say, a #$Photon - is absorbed by matter, that bit of radiation is missing from what is reflected or transmitted as the radiation strikes the matter.  Rather than being destroyed, the energy is converted to a different form: often, an increase in the excitation state of electrons in the matter. ") :- x_cid(a65b16505eff00).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eff00,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eff00,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eff00,20585).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505eff00,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eff00,[]).
x_comment(x_ElectromagneticRadiationReflection,"A specialization of #$PhysicalEvent.  Instances are events in which some quantity #$ElectromagneticRadiation, after traveling in a particular direction so as to strike some piece of matter, \"bounces\" off the surface of that matter (or off irregularities below the surface of the matter) in a new direction.  When the matter encountered forms a smooth reflective surface, reflecting can occur in a specular fashion, so that all of the outgoing radiation travels in the same, predictable direction.  This allows patterns of intensity and wavelength present in the incoming radiation to be preserved in the outgoing radiation, as when an image is reflected in a mirror.  When the surface is rough, or when features below the surface are reflective and oriented in a variety of directions (e.g., edges of cells in organic matter, or edges of crystals, etc.), radiation will be reflected in a diffuse fashion.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eff01).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eff01,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eff01,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eff01,20586).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505eff01,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eff01,[]).
x_comment(x_SpecularReflection,"A specialization of #$ElectromagneticRadiationReflection.  Instances are events in which some quantity of #$ElectromagneticRadiation, after traveling in a single direction so as to strike some piece of matter having a smooth, reflective surface, \"bounces\" off the surface of that matter. All of the outgoing radiation travels in the same, predictable direction.  This allows patterns of intensity and wavelength present in the incoming radiation to be preserved in the outgoing radiation, as when an image is reflected in a mirror.  In this type of reflection, the angle that the incident (incoming) radiation makes to the surface is the same as the angle that the reflected radiation makes to the surface, except it is on the other side of the \"surface normal\" (a vector exactly perpendicular to the surface).  The surface normal, the vector of the incident radiation and the vector of the outgoing radiation are all in the same plane.") :- x_cid(a65b16505eff02).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eff02,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505eff02,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505eff02,20587).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505eff02,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505eff02,[]).
x_comment(x_ElectromagneticRadiationStrikingMatter,"This is the collection of physical events in which some quantity of #$ElectromagneticRadiation meets with matter.  When this occurs, the EM radiation is either transmitted (see #$ElectromagneticRadiationTransmission), reflected (see #$ElectromagneticRadiationReflection) or absorbed (see #$ElectromagneticRadiationAbsorption).  It is common for a given sample of physical stuff to transmit part of the radiation, reflect part of it, and absorb part of it all at the same time, unless the radiation under consideration is an indivisible amount (a #$Photon).") :- x_cid(a65b16505eff03).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505eff03,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
