x_comment(x_approximateQuantityForInstance,"(#$approximateQuantityForInstance PRED INST QUANT) means that #$ScalarQuantity QUANT is an approximation of the actual #$ScalarPointValue of INST with respect to #$MeasurableQuantitySlot PRED. The use of #$approximateQuantityForInstance rather than (PRED INST QUANT) is taken to indicate some error in measurement, rounding, or both. When (#$approximateQuantityForInstance PRED INST QUANT) is asserted, a corresponding (#$approximateQuantityForInstance-ErrorSpecified PRED INST QUANT-POINT ERROR) will be forward concluded. If QUANT is a #$ScalarProperInterval, QUANT-POINT will be the midpoint #$ScalarPointValue of the interval, and the error will be the distance from the endpoints to this midpoint. If QUANT is a #$ScalarPointValue, QUANT-POINT = QUANT, and the estimated error will be equal to one half of the order magnitude of the smallest significant digit of QUANT (that is, if the trailing significant digit is .000X, it has a magnitude of -4, and thus the assumed maximum error is .5*10^-4, resulting in a range of .000X -.00005 to .000X + .00005). This is intended to capture the assumptions that a.) if the error was expected to be greater than this value, either a less precise value would have been provided (or #$approximateQuantityForInstance-ErrorSpecified would have been used) and b.) rounding affects, at most, the terminal digit, and at most by one half of the magnitude of that digit. For example, the assertion (#$approximateQuantityForInstance #$thicknessOfObject #$CellMembrane007 (#$Nano #$Meter 8)) will result in the corresponding assertion (#$approximateQuantityForInstance-ErrorSpecified #$thicknessOfObject #$CellMembrane007 (#$Nano #$Meter 8) (#$Nano #$Meter .5)) in the KB.  (#$approximateQuantityForInstance #$thicknessOfObject #$CellMembrane008 (#$Nano #$Meter 8 10)) would result in (#$approximateQuantityForInstance-ErrorSpecified #$thicknessOfObject #$CellMembrane008 (#$Nano #$Meter 9) (#$Nano #$Meter 1)). When error is known, use #$approximateQuantityForInstance-ErrorSpecified instead.") :- x_cid(a65b16505f0015).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505f0015,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505f0015,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505f0015,20863).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505f0015,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505f0015,[]).
x_comment('x_approximateQuantityForInstance-ErrorSpecified',"(#$approximateQuantityForInstance-ErrorSpecified PRED INST QUANT ERROR) means that QUANT is an approximation of the actual value of PRED for INST, to within ERROR. ERROR is either a known error value, supplied at assertion time, or it is estimated as a consequence of an #$approximateQuantityForInstance assertion (in which case it will have been foward concluded). For example, (approximateQuantityForInstance-ErrorSpecified objectLength Surfboard0001 (Meters 3.5) (Meters .05)) means that the actual length of Surfboard0001 is within .05 meters of 3.5 meters. The error is primarily useful in inference regarding metric comparison between individuals.") :- x_cid(a65b16505f0016).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505f0016,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505f0016,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505f0016,20864).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505f0016,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505f0016,[]).
x_comment(x_approximateQuantityForType,"(#$approximateQuantityForType PRED COL QUANT) means that #$ScalarQuantity QUANT is the approximate scalar quantity for the #$Collection COL with respect to #$MeasurableQuantitySlot PRED. The use of #$approximateQuantityForType rather than #$relationAllInstance is taken to indicate variance with respect to the actual values of individuals. If QUANT is a #$ScalarPointValue, it is taken to represent the median value of the distribution of values for instances of the collection. It would be false to infer that all instances have this value, even approximately (q.v. approximateQuantityForInstance), and in fact none of them might have this value. When QUANT is a #$ScalarProperInterval, the range specified by QUANT is taken to be the range from one standard deviation above and below the mean of the actual values of the instances. Thus, is should be expected that at least 68% of instances have values within this range. When (#$approximateQuantityForType PRED COL QUANT) is asserted, a corresponding (approximateQuantityForType-SigmaSpecified PRED COL QUANT-POINT SIGMA)  assertion is forward concluded, according to the following rules:\n\n1.) When QUANT is a #$ProperScalarInterval, QUANT-POINT is the midpoint of QUANT and SIGMA is the distance from QUANT-POINT to either end of the interval.\n\n2.) When QUANT is fractional (not an integer) or if more than half of QUANT's digits are significant, QUANT-POINT = QUANT and  SIGMA is equal to 2 times the order of magnitude of the trailing significant digit. For example, if QUANT is (Meter 2.3943), SIGMA will be (Meter .0002). If QUANT is (Meter 594380), SIGMA will be (Meter 20).\n\n3.) When QUANT is an integer with fewer than half of its digits significant, QUANT-POINT = QUANT and  SIGMA is equal to 2 times one order of magnitude lower than the trailing significant digit. For example, if QUANT is (Kilometer 1243000000), SIGMA will be (Kilometer 200000).\n\nIn all cases, SIGMA is an estimated standard deviation for the actual distribution of values over the population of COL. Obviously, it is highly unlikely that estimated SIGMAs are the *actual* standard deviations. It is hoped that these estimates are conservative enough that *AT LEAST* 95% of instances fall within (#$TimesFn SIGMA 2) of QUANT, while at the same time being narrow enough to permit metric comparisons, inferences concluding (not (isa ?X ?Y)), etc. The reason for separating cases (2) and (3) above is that integer values with few significant digits are taken to be specific up to the trailing digit. In the limiting case of a single significant digit, e.g., (Mile 1000), it would not do for SIGMA to be (Mile 2000), so we instead use (Mile 200) (still a very conservative estimate).") :- x_cid(a65b16505f0017).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505f0017,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505f0017,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505f0017,20865).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505f0017,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505f0017,[]).
x_comment('x_approximateQuantityForType-SigmaSpecified',"(#$approximateQuantityForType PRED COL QUANT SIGMA) means that #$ScalarQuantity QUANT is the approximate scalar quantity for the #$Collection COL with respect to #$MeasurableQuantitySlot PRED. That is, QUANT is taken to be the mean of the distribution of values related to instances of COL via PRED, and SIGMA is taken to be the standard deviation of this distribution (and may have been estimated via a #$approximateQuantityForType assertion). This facilitates reasoning that requires comparison between types that admit of variance. For example, if (#$approximateQuantityForType-SigmaSpecified PRED COL1 QUANT1 SIGMA1) and (approximateQuantityForType-SigmaSpecified PRED COL2 QUANT2 SIGMA2), and the difference between QUANT1 and QUANT2 is greater than (#$PlusFn (#$TimesFn SIGMA1 3) (#$TimesFn SIGMA2 3)), it is safe to concluded that (disjointWith COL1 COL2).") :- x_cid(a65b16505f0018).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505f0018,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
