x_comment(x_TotalityOfSubEventsOfTypeFn,"<code>(#$TotalityOfSubEventsOfTypeFn EVENT-TYPE SUB-EVENT-TYPE)</code> denotes the collection of #$IteratedEventScenes that are #$properSubEventTypes of <code>EVENT-TYPE</code> that satisfy the following conditions <pre> Assume TOTALITY = (#$TotalityOfSubEventsOfTypeFn EVENT-TYPE SUB-EVENT-TYPE) <br><br>   1.  <b>Maximality</b>: If EVENT is an instance of EVENT-TYPE and TOTAL-01 is a proper sub-event of<br> EVENT and an instance of TOTALITY, then for any SUB such that SUB is a<br> sub-event of EVENT and an instance of SUB-EVENT-TYPE, SUB is a sub-event<br> of TOTAL-01  <br> <br> 2.  <b>Exclusion</b>: Any sub-event of an instance of the totality collection is a sub-event of<br> some instance of SUB-EVENT-TYPE or some temporal sum of them -- i.e. every part of<br> it is a sub-event of some instance of SUB-EVENT-TYPE.  </pre>  For example, assume DrawingWaterFromAManualPump is a complex event type in which a bucket or other container is placed under a manual water pump, optionally some water is poured into the pump to prime it, then a number of iterations of MovingTheHandleOfAManualPumpUpAndDown are performed until water begins to flow into the container.  Given that, (#$TotalityOfSubEventsOfTypeFn DrawingWaterFromAManualPump MovingTheHandleOfAManualPumpUpAndDown) is the collection of the proper sub-events of instances of DrawingWaterFromAManualPump that consist of repeated instances of MovingTheHandleOfAManualPumpUpAndDown.  It is easy to see that any sub-event of an instance of (#$TotalityOfSubEventsOfTypeFn DrawingWaterFromAManualPump MovingTheHandleOfAManualPumpUpAndDown) is itself a sub-event of some instance of MovingTheHandleOfAManualPumpUpAndDown -- e.g. the first time the handle is moved upwards, or is a sub-event of some sum -- e.g. the arbitrary sub-event that consists of moving the handle down during the second iteration and moving the handle up during the third iteration is a sub-event of the sum of the second and third iterations.  <br><br>Note that the instances of the returned collection need not be temporally contiguous (cf. contiguousAfter).  E.g. each instance of (#$TotalityOfSubEventsOfTypeFn #$BaseballGame #$BaseballInning) consists of a series of temporally non-contiguous baseball innings.  Given that the events between the innings are not sub-events of the innings, it is easy to see how those portions of the baseball game fail to be subevents of instances of this collection -- the Exclusion condition rules them out because no part of any break between the innings is a part of a baseball inning. This is the function that corresponds to the third argument of the predicate #$iterativeSubEventTypesTotality.") :- x_cid(a65b16505f00c8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505f00c8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505f00c8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505f00c8,21074).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505f00c8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505f00c8,[]).
