x_comment(x_SimpleRedoxReaction,"A specialization of #$RedoxReaction and an instance of #$TemporalObjectType.  Instances of #$SimpleRedoxReaction are #$ChemicalReactions in which #$Electrons are transferred from one #$ChemicalObject to another.  The object providing the electrons is oxidized (see #$Oxidation-HalfReaction), while the one gaining the electrons is reduced (see #$Reduction-HalfReaction).  \n<p>In some cases entire electrons change ownership. For example, when #$Sodium reacts with #$Chlorine to form #$Salt-NaCl, an #$Electron from #$Sodium is transferred wholly to the atom of #$Chlorine.   In other cases the degree of electron sharing between atoms changes. For example, in the #$Carbon to #$Hydrogen bonds in #$Methane, a pair of electrons is shared about equally; the same is true of molecular #$Oxygen (O2).  After methane and oxygen react to form CO2 and water, electrons move closer to the oxygen atoms, but they don't entirely leave the carbon atom (now part of CO2) or the hydrogen atom (now part of H2O).\n<p>#$SimpleRedoxReaction instances complete in a single step, having one #$Reduction-HalfReaction, one #$Oxidation-HalfReaction and one #$ElectronTransfer as subevents.  Complex redox reactions needing muliple steps to complete, such as the oxidation of organic molecules in #$CellularRespiration, are not included in this collection.") :- x_cid(a65b16505f00d9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505f00d9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505f00d9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505f00d9,21102).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505f00d9,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505f00d9,[]).
