x_comment(x_situationFocalLocation,"<code>(#$situationFocalLocation SIT LOCATION)</code> means that the #$Situation, <code>SIT</code>, is focally located at <code>LOCATION</code>, an instance of #$EnduringThing-Localized.  #$situationFocalLocation is more restrictive than its generalization, #$situationLocation, which applies to all instances of #$EnduringThing-Localized that spatially subsume every object to which it applies.  I.e., given an instance of #$ScrewedConnection, <code>ScrewedConnection-13926</code> that occurs within a piece of furniture, <code>Table-43</code>, #$situationLocation will not only hold between <code>ScrewedConnection-13926</code> and <code>Table-43</code>, it will hold of <code>ScrewedConnection-13926</code> and the room in which <code>Table-43</code> resides, the floor of the building where the room is, the building itself, ..., the planet Earth, and the entire physical universe.  However, #$situationFocalLocation holds only of <code>ScrewedConnection-13926</code> and <code>Table-43</code>.  This is not to say that #$situationFocalLocation is functional or single-entry in its second argument place; depending on the granularity of the context, it might be that the #$situationFocalLocation of a situation might include a range of locations.  However, unlike #$situationLocation, #$situationFocalLocation does not propagate to spatially-subsuming locations of its second argument. ") :- x_cid(a65b16505f02e5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505f02e5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505f02e5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
