x_comment(x_ist(x_UniversalVocabularyMt,x_not(x_genls(x_Communicating,x_InformationTransferEvent))),"The required roles for #$InformationTransferEvent include                                     #$informationOrigin and #$informationDestination, which are negation                          preds.                                                                                                                                                                                      If #$Communicating were a spec of #$InformationTransferEvent, #$informationOrigin and #$informationDestination would become                       required roles -- so in a group discussion, someone would *have* to play                      the #$informationOrigin role, and that someone could not also play the                        #$informationDestination role.  Similarly, someone would *have* to play                       the #$informationDestination role without playing the #$informationOrigin                      role.                                                                                                                                                                                       <p>By having:                                                                                                                                                                                   <pre>\n   (#$genls #$CommunicationAct-Single #$InformationTransferEvent)\n   (#$subEventTypes #$Communicating #$CommunicationAct-Single)</pre>                                                                                                                                </code>      we were able to avoid this problem, while maintaining the ability to                          represent that there are #$informationOrigin and #$informationDestination                     role-players *during* any communcating event, in the sense that there are                     such role players *in* the individual info-transfers that are parts of the                    communciating event.                                                                                                                                                                        <p>The negation was added to draw cyclists' attention to the problem, to help them avoid succumbing to the temptation of adding the genls link, thereby re-introducing the                          \nrequired roles problem.") :- x_cid(a65b16505f05ea).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505f05ea,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505f05ea,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16505f05ea,22631).
xc_kb_names(a65b16505f05ea,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16505f05ea,[]).
x_comment(x_ist(x_UniversalVocabularyMt,x_not(x_disjointWith(x_AsymmetricBinaryPredicate,x_SymmetricBinaryPredicate))),"Although probably no reified predicate should ever be an instance of both \n#$SymmetricBinaryPredicate and #$AsymmetricBinaryPredicate, note that \nthese collections are not strictly disjoint.  Since both collections \nare defined with respect to the condition (on a predicate PRED) that \n(PRED THING1 THING2) holds (for a given THING1 and THING2), a binary\npredicate with a necessarily empty extension is trivially both \nsymmetric and asymmetric.  For example, a predicate that was (aptly)\nnamed 'equalToAndDifferent' would likely be of this sort.  Still, it \nis not clear what practical reason there could be to individually \nrepresent any such predicate in the Cyc Knowledge Base.") :- x_cid(a65b16505f05eb).
xc_microtheory(a65b16505f05eb,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16505f05eb,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part07.krf').
